\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1.5 \u2014 BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nStep 1.5-1. Base Rates\n- 5-year survival probability for a large-cap, net-cash beverage company\n  with 25%+ ROE: >95% [Assumption based on Fama-French/CRSP history of\n  large-cap consumer staples]\n- A $8\u20139B-revenue company sustaining >10% revenue growth for 5 years:\n  ~35\u201340% [Assumption \u2014 base rates of long-run growth deceleration]\n- TAM-narrative actual reach (category forecasts achieved): historically low,\n  ~30\u201340% [Assumption \u2014 GMI-type category forecasts routinely miss]\n- Adjacent-brand acquisition generating ROIC > WACC: ~25\u201335% for large\n  beverage M&A [Estimate \u2014 academic base rate]\n- Platform/brand transition (core brand extending into new categories):\n  ~20\u201330% [Estimate \u2014 new-product-line base rates]\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation of This Analysis from Base Rate\nMy Bull case (30% weight) requires MNST to sustain double-digit growth for\n5+ years \u2014 above the ~35\u201340% base rate. Specific justifications for the\ndeviation:\n 1. International is 46% of sales and growing 34.6% YoY (EMEA +27.2%,\n    APAC +35.7%, LatAm +56.1%) [Actual \u2014 Q2 call]; the company is a\n    share-gainer (+220bps EMEA value share, +70bps US) [Actual]\n 2. Coca-Cola distribution partnership provides structural international\n    infrastructure that most growth companies lack [Actual \u2014 Q2 call]\n 3. Zero-sugar sub-brand (Ultra +19%, EU zero-sugar +23%, 44.5% EU value\n    share) is capturing the fastest-growing consumer segment [Actual]\nDeviation is modest (30% vs ~35\u201340% base rate) \u2014 no red flag.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base-Rate Shock Check\n- Bull probability 30% < 1.5\u00d7 base rate (no additional evidence required)\n- 5-year survival >95% is consistent with base rates for this class.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nStep 2-1. Business Understanding\nMonster develops, markets, and distributes energy drinks (Monster Energy,\nUltra, Reign, Bang, Predator, Fury) and strategic beverage brands (teas,\njuices, coffee) plus a small alcohol segment. It operates four segments:\nMonster Energy Drinks ($2.36B in Q2-26, +21.6% YoY), Strategic Brands\n($143.7M, +10.6%), Alcohol Brands ($32.2M, -15.2%) and Other. Distribution\nruns through Coca-Cola's global bottling network and a DSD model in the US.\nUnderstandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat Assessment (0\u20133 each)\nIntangible assets/brand          3  \u2014 top-2 global energy brand, ~39% share\nSwitching costs                  1  \u2014 habitual, but not locked-in\nNetwork effects                  1  \u2014 Coke bottler ecosystem, shelf density\nCost advantage                   2  \u2014 scale in aluminum/sugar procurement\nEconomies of scale               2  \u2014 marketing spread over $9B revenue\nRegulatory barriers              1  \u2014 moderate, no license protection\nData moat                        0  \u2014 limited proprietary data advantage\nMOAT SCORE: 10/21 = NARROW MOAT\n\nMoat direction: STABLE \u2014 widening in EMEA (+220bps, zero-sugar leadership),\nUS (+70bps share); narrowing in alcohol segment and full-sugar amid\nCelsius/Alani Nu/C4 competition. Net: stable.\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch Classification\nFAST GROWER \u2014 10-year median operating margin 32.9%, revenue CAGR +12.5%\n(5-yr), EPS compounding in the mid-teens. Core checkpoints: P/E relative to\ngrowth is demanding; balance sheet strong; franchise shows pricing power\n(Q4 2026 US pricing actions announced [Actual \u2014 Q2 call]).\n\nStep 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard\nItem                                FY2025            Rating\nRevenue growth (3-yr CAGR)          +9.5% [Arithmetic]  Good\nRevenue growth (TTM, Q2-26)         +20.4% [Actual]     Excellent\nOperating margin (FY25 / median)    29.2% / 32.9%       Excellent\nROE (TTM)                            25.7% [Vendor]      Excellent\nFCF margin                          $1.966B/$8.294B=23.7% Excellent\nFCF conversion vs net income        103%                Excellent\nNet cash                            ~$2.4\u20132.8B [Est.]   Excellent\nInterest coverage                   N/A (net cash)      Excellent\n\nVERDICT: EXCELLENT financial strength.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard (0\u20133 each, max 15)\nBuyback efficiency              2 \u2014 shares -7% over 2 years (2023\u201325,\n                                    split-adjusted); $900M remaining\n                                    authorization Aug 5, 2026 [Actual]\nM&A track record                1 \u2014 alcohol acquisitions are declining\n                                    (-15.2% YoY); Bang appears OK; mixed\nCapex efficiency                3 \u2014 capex 1.6% of revenue, incremental\n                                    ROIC >30% [Estimate]\nRationality of mix              2 \u2014 heavy buybacks, no dividend, SBC modest\nGuidance accuracy               2 \u2014 beats revenue, slight EPS miss headline\n                                    (Q2-26), no formal guidance issued\nSubtotal: 10/15\nInsider ownership bonus         +1 \u2014 insider ownership 28.3% [Aggregator]\nTOTAL: 11/15\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2.5 \u2014 INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nStep 2.5-1 R&D Capitalization: Immaterial \u2014 beverage companies carry minimal\nR&D; formulation spend embedded in SG&A. No adjustment.\n\nStep 2.5-2 SBC Treatment: FY2025 SBC $0.126B [Actual]. Operating income\nalready reflects SBC as an expense (it is inside SG&A), so FCFF is not\ndouble-subtracted; but owner-FCF measured from OCF must deduct it:\nAdjusted FCF (from OCF) = OCF $2.098B \u2212 capex $0.132B \u2212 SBC $0.126B\n                        = $1.840B [Arithmetic]\n\nStep 2.5-3 Operating Leases: FY24 operating lease $0.056B [Actual];\nimmaterial \u2014 no adjustment.\n\nStep 2.5-4 Brand Asset: Monster is a top-2 global energy brand (est. $5\u20138B\nbrand value, Interbrand-method approximation) [Estimate]. Brand value is\nalready captured in gross-margin and volume performance; no separate\ncapitalization in the DCF.\n\nStep 2.5-5 Adjusted ROIC:\nNOPAT FY25 = $2.419B \u00d7 (1 \u2212 0.239) = $1.841B [Estimate \u2014 tax from Q2 23.9%]\nInvested capital = Equity $8.254B \u2212 cash $2.088B \u2212 ST investments $0.677B\n                 + debt \u2248 $0.374B = $5.863B [Arithmetic]\nAdjusted ROIC \u2248 $1.841B / $5.863B = 31.4%\nGAAP ROE = 25.7%; gap explained by net cash being excluded from IC.\nBoth confirm a high-quality, self-funding franchise.\n\nStep 2.5-6 Owner Earnings (Buffett):\nNI $1.905B + D&A $0.114B \u2212 maintenance capex ~$0.080B \u2212 \u0394non-cash WC $0.137B\n= $1.802B [Estimate]\n(\u0394non-cash WC is computed on receivables+inventory, excluding cash/STI:\nFY25: ($5.361\u2212$2.088\u2212$0.677)\u2212$1.448 = $1.148B vs FY24:\n($3.642\u2212$1.533\u2212$0.000)\u2212$1.098 = $1.011B \u2192 \u0394 = +$0.137B [Arithmetic])\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION (ENGINE 1: DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2