rate sensitivity: \u22122 (43x P/E means duration-like risk; net cash only partially offsets)\n  \u2022 Dollar sensitivity: \u22121 (46% of Q2'26 sales international; a weaker dollar [\u2212] is a tailwind)\n  \u2022 Liquidity sensitivity: \u22121 (late-cycle; high-quality staples already crowded)\n  \u2022 Economic-cycle sensitivity: \u22121 (recession-resistant, but energy drinks are discretionary-adjacent)\n  \u2022 Commodity sensitivity: \u22121 (aluminum cans; tariffs boosting costs through 2026 per Q2'26 call)\n\n\u25a0 1-4 Macro alignment: Probability-weighted impact = \u22121.2 \u2192 MILD HEADWIND\n  The high rate/high-for-longer regime is a headwind for a 43x P/E \"bond proxy\" staple.\n  The dollar's moderate weakness is a tailwind for reported international revenue.\n  Net: conviction downgrade of 0.5 steps on entry timing; no change to intrinsic value.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\n\u25a0 1.5-1 Base rates applied to MNST:\n  \u2022 5-year survival for an established profitable beverage company: >95% (base rate)\n  \u2022 Probability a quality staple generates >10% revenue CAGR over a decade: ~25\u201335% [Assumption]\n  \u2022 Probability a consumer brand with 39% global share INCREASES share for 10 more years: ~30%\n  \u2022 Large M&A (>$1B) delivering ROIC > WACC within 5 years: ~30% \u2014 applies to the acquired\n    alcohol segment (Q2'26 alcohol sales $32.2M, \u221215.2% y/y)\n  \u2022 Sustained 40x+ P/E for consumer staples reverting to \u226425x over 5 years: ~50\u201360%\n\n\u25a0 1.5-2 Deviation from base rate:\n  My base case assumes MNST grows revenue at ~8.6% CAGR over FY2025\u20132035, slightly ABOVE the\n  global energy drink category CAGR of ~8.2% (EIN News/industry analysis, 2026). This deviation is\n  justified by:\n  1. Coke bottling network expansion in Southeast Asia, China, India, and EMEA \u2014 an [Actual] channel\n     advantage (Q2'26 transcript: APAC +35.7%, China +62.5%, India +84%, Brazil +82%)\n  2. Monster brand value-share gains: US +70bps, EMEA +220bps in Q2'26 [Actual company disclosure]\n  3. Innovation pipeline (Ultra +19%, Juice +26% in the US) sustaining shelf velocity\n  I am NOT deviating above 1.5x base rate: my 8.6% growth is roughly in line with category growth\n  plus modest share gains, not a step-change.\n\n\u25a0 1.5-3 Base rate shock check: PASS.\n  My success probability (\u224845% for base case FV) is not >1.5x the base rate for the scenario.\n  However, the MARKET price implies >11% growth for a decade \u2014 a scenario I assign only ~20\u201325%\n  probability. The market is pricing a 1.5\u20132.0x deviation from category growth base rates.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY (Buffett + Lynch)\n\n\u25a0 2-1 Business understanding:\n  Monster develops and markets energy drinks (Monster Energy, Ultra, Juice, Java, Reign), with\n  distribution largely through Coca-Cola bottlers. It sells concentrates to bottlers and finished\n  products to distributors/retailers. It also owns small strategic/affordable brands (Predator,\n  Fury) and a declining alcohol segment. Understandability: HIGH.\n\n\u25a0 2-2 Moat assessment (0\u20133 each; total /21):\n  \u2022 Intangible assets (brand): 3 \u2014 Monster is #2 globally (~39% share vs Red Bull ~43%) [Estimate \u2014\n    CSImarket via research brief, 2026]; brand is iconic in the action-sports subculture\n  \u2022 Switching costs: 1 \u2014 energy drink consumers are habit-prone but not locked in\n  \u2022 Network effects: 0 \u2014 none\n  \u2022 Cost advantage: 3 \u2014 global scale procurement, Coke distribution economics\n  \u2022 Economies of scale: 2 \u2014 strong but Red Bull is comparable\n  \u2022 Regulatory barriers: 2 \u2014 global food/beverage compliance infrastructure\n  \u2022 Data moat: 1 \u2014 consumer insights (minuscule)\n  MOAT SCORE: 12/21 \u2192 WIDE MOAT (lower band). Direction: STABLE-to-WIDENING internationally\n  (Coke distribution opening new markets) but NARROWING in the US (Celsius/Pepsi, private label).\n\n\u25a0 2-3 Lynch classification: FAST GROWER \u2192 STALWART TRANSITION.\n  Core checkpoints: (a) sustainability of growth \u2014 YES, supported by international volume;\n  (b) balance-sheet strength \u2014 YES net cash; (c) can the company fund its own growth \u2014 YES;\n  (d) risk of growth stall \u2014 YES, US share is high and category growth is moderating.\n\n\u25a0 2-4 Financial strength scorecard:\n  \u2022 Revenue growth (3-yr CAGR FY22-FY25): +9.5% [Arithmetic]; last 4Q y/y: +20.4% [Arithmetic]\n  \u2022 Operating margin (FY25 / 3-yr avg): 29.2% / ~27.5% [Arithmetic]\n  \u2022 EPS growth (FY22\u2192FY25 NI CAGR): +16.9% [Arithmetic]\n  \u2022 ROE: 25.7% [Aggregator]; ROIC \u2248 31% [Estimate]\n  \u2022 FCF margin: 23.7% [Arithmetic]; FCF/OCF conversion: 93.7% [Arithmetic]\n  \u2022 Net debt: NET CASH \u2248 $2.33B [Estimate \u2014 see Section 3 debt components]\n  \u2022 Piotroski F-score: 5/7 [Pack] \u2014 pass\n  \u2022 Accruals ratio: \u22120.02 [Pack] \u2014 high earnings quality\n  JUDGMENT: EXCELLENT\n\n\u25a0 2-5 Capital allocation scorecard (0\u201315):\n  1. Buyback efficiency: 1/3 \u2014 $900M authorization (Q2'26 transcript) but at 40x earnings it is\n     value-destructive; shares outstanding flat, consistent with SBC offset, not net retirement\n  2. M&A track record: 1/3 \u2014 alcohol segment is shrinking (\u221215.2% y/y) and immaterial at $32M/qtr;\n     ROIC appears < WACC [Estimate]\n  3. Capex efficiency: 3/3 \u2014 capex/to-revenue \u2248 1.4%; incremental ROIC far above WACC\n  4. Rationality of mix: 2/3 \u2014 hoarding ~$2.8B cash/investments earning low yields is suboptimal;\n     the FY2023 retained-earnings drop of $3.06B [Unconfirmed \u2014 see Audit] suggests a large\n     distribution, which would be rational if real\n  5. Guidance accuracy: 2/3 \u2014 2025's Q1 US weakness was not flagged; otherwise consistent\n  SUBTOTAL: 9/15\n  INSIDER OWNERSHIP BONUS: insiders hold ~28.3% [Aggregator] \u2192 +1 point\n  TOTAL: 10/15 \u2192 GOOD\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2.5. ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\n\u25a0 2.5-1 R&D: immaterial (beverage R&D not separately disclosed; treat as expensed) [Assumption]\n\u25a0 2.5-2 SBC: FY2025 SBC $0.126B = 1.5% of revenue [Filed]. Already expensed in EBIT.\n  Adjusted FCF = OCF \u2212 capex \u2212 SBC = 2.098 \u2212 0.132 \u2212 0.126 = $1.84B [Arithmetic]\n  Diluted post-split shares: 1,958,980,000 [Aggregator] \u2014 consistent with the 2:1 split.\n\u25a0 2.5-3 Leases: FY2024 operating leases $0.056B + finance leases $0.004B [Filed] \u2014 immaterial.\n\u25a0 2.5-4 Brand value: not independently estimated; the P&L shows the brand's value (55.8% GM).\n\u25a0 2.5-5 Adjusted ROIC = NOPAT / (equity + debt \u2212 cash \u2212 ST investments)\n  = 1.839 / (8.254 + 0.434 \u2212 2.088 \u2212 0.677) = 1.839 / 5.923 = 31.0% [Estimate]\n  GAAP ROIC \u2248 26% \u2192 gap \u2248 5pts, explained by net cash drag and lease capitalization. Both are strong.\n\u25a0 2.5-6 Owner earnings (Buffett) \u2248 NI $1.905 + D&A $0.114 \u2212 maintenance capex $0.090 [Assumption]\n  \u2212 \u0394OWC $0.320 [Estimate] = $1.61B.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3. VALUATION (ENGINE 1 \u2014 DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\n\u25a0 3-1 Discount rate derivation:\n  \u2022 Risk-free rate: 4.68% [Actual \u2014 10Y Treasury, Aug 14, 2026]\n  \u2022 Equity risk premium: 5.0% [Assumption]\n  \u2022 Beta: 0.70 [Assumption \u2014 vendor's 0.524 is unverified and likely too low (it implies an 8.5%\n    cost of capital for a consumer staple at 46% international); 0.70 reflects defensive category,\n    net cash, but also regulatory/crowding risk]\n  \u2022 Cos