lready reflects SBC as an expense (it is inside SG&A), so FCFF is not\ndouble-subtracted; but owner-FCF measured from OCF must deduct it:\nAdjusted FCF (from OCF) = OCF $2.098B \u2212 capex $0.132B \u2212 SBC $0.126B\n                        = $1.840B [Arithmetic]\n\nStep 2.5-3 Operating Leases: FY24 operating lease $0.056B [Actual];\nimmaterial \u2014 no adjustment.\n\nStep 2.5-4 Brand Asset: Monster is a top-2 global energy brand (est. $5\u20138B\nbrand value, Interbrand-method approximation) [Estimate]. Brand value is\nalready captured in gross-margin and volume performance; no separate\ncapitalization in the DCF.\n\nStep 2.5-5 Adjusted ROIC:\nNOPAT FY25 = $2.419B \u00d7 (1 \u2212 0.239) = $1.841B [Estimate \u2014 tax from Q2 23.9%]\nInvested capital = Equity $8.254B \u2212 cash $2.088B \u2212 ST investments $0.677B\n                 + debt \u2248 $0.374B = $5.863B [Arithmetic]\nAdjusted ROIC \u2248 $1.841B / $5.863B = 31.4%\nGAAP ROE = 25.7%; gap explained by net cash being excluded from IC.\nBoth confirm a high-quality, self-funding franchise.\n\nStep 2.5-6 Owner Earnings (Buffett):\nNI $1.905B + D&A $0.114B \u2212 maintenance capex ~$0.080B \u2212 \u0394non-cash WC $0.137B\n= $1.802B [Estimate]\n(\u0394non-cash WC is computed on receivables+inventory, excluding cash/STI:\nFY25: ($5.361\u2212$2.088\u2212$0.677)\u2212$1.448 = $1.148B vs FY24:\n($3.642\u2212$1.533\u2212$0.000)\u2212$1.098 = $1.011B \u2192 \u0394 = +$0.137B [Arithmetic])\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION (ENGINE 1: DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nDISCOUNT RATE DERIVATION\nRisk-free rate (10Y UST)          4.71% [Actual \u2014 Trading Economics]\nRaw beta (vendor)                 0.524 [Actual \u2014 vendor, window unstated]\nAdjusted beta (2/3 raw + 1/3 \u00d7 1) 0.68 [Assumption]\nEquity risk premium               4.50% [Assumption \u2014 Damodaran range 4\u20135%]\nCost of equity = 4.71% + 0.68\u00d74.5% = 7.77% [Arithmetic]\nDebt weight                       5% (net-cash; capacity) [Assumption]\nCost of debt after-tax            5.0%\u00d7(1\u22120.24)=3.80% [Assumption]\nWACC = 0.95\u00d77.77% + 0.05\u00d73.80%    = 7.57% \u2192 USE 7.5% [Assumption]\n\nA 7.5% WACC is intentionally non-punitive. If one insisted on beta=1.0,\nCoE would be 9.2% and fair value falls to ~$25\u201328/share \u2014 meaning our base\ncase is not setting the value low by using an aggressive discount rate.\n\nFORECAST DRIVER SOURCING\nRevenue growth:\n  FY2026: +18.1% to $9.80B \u2014 H1-26 actuals $4.893B (+23.3% YoY)\n          [Actual\u201410-Q]; July +14.3% [Actual\u2014Q2 call]; H2 path estimated\n          to decelerate [Assumption]\n  FY2027: +10.2% \u2014 sell-side consensus forward revenue growth +9.46%\n          [Estimate\u2014vendor]; slight uplift for FX/mix [Assumption]\n  FY2028\u201332: glide 9.0% to 5.0% \u2014 global category CAGR ~7.7% [Estimate\u2014\n          GMI Research, 2021\u21922029]; MNST gaining share but base gets large;\n          TAM forecasts historically overstate reach [Assumption]\nMargins:\n  FY2026 op margin 29.5% \u2014 Q2-26 actual 29.2% [Actual]; Q4 pricing actions\n          and leverage [Assumption]\n  FY2027\u201330: 30.0% to 31.5% \u2014 recovers toward 10-yr median 32.9%\n          [Actual 10-yr median; path is an Assumption]\n  Terminal 31.5% [Assumption]\nTax rate: 24.0% (Q2-26 effective 23.9%) [Actual\u2192Extrapolation]\nCapex: 2.0% of revenue (FY25 1.6% actual; SAP S/4HANA go-live Jan 1 2028\n          requires some uplift, then normalizes) [Assumption]\nD&A: 1.4% of revenue (FY25 actual 1.4%) [Assumption]\n\u0394WC: 1.5% of incremental revenue (normalized from lumpy receivables swings)\n          [Assumption]\nTerminal growth: 3.0% \u2014 below long-run nominal GDP; energy-drink volume\n          growth \u2248 population + modest per-capita + pricing \u2248 2.5\u20133.5%\n          [Assumption]\n\nDCF TABLE ($ billions)\nYear   Rev    OpMgn  EBIT   NOPAT  D&A   Capex  \u0394WC    FCF      PV@7.5%\n2026   9.80   29.5%  2.891  2.197  0.137 0.196  0.023  2.115    1.967\n2027   10.80  30.0%  3.240  2.462  0.151 0.216  0.015  2.382    2.061\n2028   11.77  30.5%  3.590  2.728  0.165 0.235  0.015  2.643    2.128\n2029   12.71  31.0%  3.940  2.994  0.178 0.254  0.014  2.904    2.174\n2030   13.58  31.5%  4.278  3.251  0.190 0.272  0.013  3.156    2.199\n2031   14.38  31.5%  4.530  3.443  0.201 0.288  0.012  3.344    2.167\n2032   15.10  31.5%  4.757  3.615  0.211 0.302  0.011  3.513    2.117\nSUM PV FCF 2026\u20132032                                           14.814\n\nTerminal value = 3.513\u00d71.03/(0.075\u22120.03) = 80.41\nPV of terminal value = 80.41 \u00d7 0.6027 = 48.47\nEnterprise value = 14.81 + 48.47 = 63.28\nNet cash = cash $2.088B + ST investments $0.677B \u2212 LT debt (FY24 $0.374B;\nFY25 not available; vendor says $0.000B total debt) \u2248 $2.4B [Estimate \u2014\nconservative; could be $2.77B]\nEquity value = $65.7B\nShares = 1.95898B\nINTRINSIC VALUE (BASE) = $33.50 per share\n\nSENSITIVITY OF BASE IV\nTerminal growth 2.5% / 3.0% / 3.5%  \u2192  $29.9 / $33.5 / $38.2\nWACC 7.0% / 7.5% / 8.0%             \u2192  $38.2 / $33.5 / $29.5\nFY27\u201330 revenue growth \u22122pts/+2pts  \u2192  $28.5 / $33.5 / $39.9\nTerminal margin 29% / 31.5% / 33%   \u2192  $30.8 / $33.5 / $35.9\n\nTORNADO TOP-3 (Thesis KPIs): Terminal growth rate; Revenue CAGR FY27\u201330;\nWACC (a proxy for the market re-rating consumer staples).\n\nREVERSE DCF ($47.79): To justify today's price with WACC 7.5%, TG 3.0%\nand my 2026\u201332 FCFs, the terminal FCF must be ~$5.7B (vs $3.5B in base).\nThat implies FY2033 revenue of ~$25B at a 31.5% margin \u2014 a 14% CAGR for\neight years. With a category CAGR of ~7.7%, this requires international\n(46% of sales) to compound at ~18% through 2033 (~17B revenue) and US at\n~5%. That is a full-bull-case outcome \u2014 possible, but a high bar.\n\nMULTIPLES CROSS-CHECK\nCurrent: Trailing P/E 43.35 [Actual\u2014vendor]; Forward P/E ~39.6 on forward\nEPS ~$1.23 [Estimate]; P/B 9.79; PEG 2.74 [Actual\u2014vendor].\nMy base IV implies a forward P/E on FY27E EPS of ~25\u201327\u00d7 \u2014 a reasonable\n\"quality compounder\" multiple, but the market is paying 40\u00d7+ for the\noptionality. No peer-multiple-only conclusion; DCF carries the weight.\n\nCONCLUSION: Base intrinsic value $33.50; 50% CI $27\u2013$41; 80% CI $19\u2013$54.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n3.5-1 Competitive Response: If MNST keeps gaining +70bps US/+220bps EMEA\nshare, Red Bull, Celsius, and Pepsi will respond with innovation and\npromotions. Celsius already rebuilt itself into a meaningful US challenger;\nPepsi's Celsius distribution partnership and Red Bull's premium brand\nposition are structural constraints on MNST's US pricing power (net impact\non base case: -1 out of +3/\u22123).\n\n3.5-2 Reflexivity: High stock price \u2192 SBC is lucrative, strong M&A\ncurrency, and the Coke alliance deepens; a falling stock \u2192 talent flight,\nbut the 28.3% insider ownership is a stabilizing force. Net: mildly\npositive in the bull case; neutral-to-negative in the bear case.\n\n3.5-3 Customer Evolution: Energy drinks have crossed from early adopters\nto mainstream (Gen Z, female recruits at 2\u00d7 category rate [Actual\u2014Q2\ncall]); LTV/CAC dynamics are favorable as zero-sugar (Ultra +19%) scales.\nThis supports my Bull case, but late-adopter economics compress pricing\npower over time \u2014 a reason my terminal growth is only 3.0%.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u25